WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.380
Access-token lifetime should balance usability, security, operational cost, and revocation needs.

2
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:15.800
Short lifetimes reduce how long an exposed access token remains useful, but they can increase refresh activity and operational complexity.

3
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:21.500
Long lifetimes reduce friction but make response and policy changes slower to take effect.

4
00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:29.460
Refresh tokens are higher-value credentials because they may allow continued access beyond a short-lived access token.

5
00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:39.560
They need explicit storage, binding, expiration, rotation, revocation, monitoring, and owner expectations.

6
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.260
A refresh token should not be treated like ordinary application state.

7
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:49.220
Rotation can reduce the value of stale refresh tokens when implemented and monitored carefully.

8
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:58.120
In a rotation model, a client receives a new refresh token as part of the refresh flow, and the previous value is retired according to policy.

9
00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:04.780
This requires consistent storage, clear failure handling, and visibility into unusual refresh behavior.

10
00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:14.580
Logout, password change, MFA change, device removal, user disablement, and suspected compromise should trigger token lifecycle review.

11
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:24.520
The right action may include invalidating sessions, revoking refresh tokens, shortening lifetimes, requiring reauthentication, or notifying owners depending on the system.

12
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:30.160
Revocation state must be available where validation or refresh decisions are made.

13
00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:37.140
A revocation decision that exists only in one application instance may not protect a distributed system.

14
00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:44.360
Multi-instance deployments, caches, gateways, and identity providers all need to participate in the intended behavior.

15
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.800
Long-lived sessions should have ownership, monitoring, and user-facing management where appropriate.

16
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:59.220
Users and administrators may need to see active devices, connected applications, or integration sessions so stale access can be removed deliberately.
